E AX3066 macro-record-diagnostics.ax:1:8-14 macro-literal "macro `unused` declares the literal `dead` but no pattern spells it" !"a declared literal that matches nothing is dead text, and the likeliest reading is a typo for a name the patterns spell - in which case the rule binds where the author meant to match" ?"spell it in a pattern, or drop it from the header"
E AX3034 macro-record-diagnostics.ax:1:8-15 macro-ellipsis "macro `repeats` has a rule with more than one `...`" !"a repeating element absorbs every argument left after the fixed ones (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-16), so a second has nothing defined to absorb and where the split fell would be the expander's guess" ?"keep one repeating element per rule; the arguments a second one would take are the ones the first already took"
E AX3033 macro-record-diagnostics.ax:1:8-14 macro-unreachable-rule "macro `shadow` has a rule that can never match: rule 2 is shadowed by rule 1" !"rules are tried IN ORDER and the first whose pattern matches wins (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-18); an earlier rule of the same arity whose elements are all plain binders matches every invocation of that arity, so nothing of that arity after it is reachable" ?"give the earlier rule a pattern that can FAIL - a literal, or a parenthesised shape of two or more elements - or delete this one. A one-element `(p)` is the bare binder `p`: the parentheses are the same ones an argument loses, so it still matches everything"
E AX3027 macro-record-diagnostics.ax:2:27-32 declaration-macro "the rule of macro `mixed` that matches here generates declarations, so it can't stand in expression position" !"each rule's template is declarations or one expression, and where the macro is invoked picks which it needs (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-14)" ?"invoke it at top level, or give the macro an earlier rule for this shape whose template is one expression"
E AX3018 macro-record-diagnostics.ax:2:27-31 macro-arity "no rule of macro `only` matches this invocation; it accepts (only 1)" !"rules are tried IN ORDER and the first whose pattern matches wins (macro-system.md MAC-LANG-18): an invocation matching none is this refusal rather than a fall-through. A plain identifier in a shape matches any argument; a literal matches its own VALUE, and a parenthesised shape matches a form of that many elements - but not a form headed by a keyword (`if`, `let`, `lambda`, `match`), which the parser gives a shape of its own" ?"write the invocation in one of the shapes above"
